# SCRIPT: txn_Rollback
# FOLDER: 00_APP
# ROLE: discard every uncommitted write since txn_Begin
# CALLED BY: any routine that hit an error between txn_Begin and txn_Commit
# CALLS: nothing
# INPUTS: $p (JSON, optional) — { "reason":<opt> } for the log line
# GLOBALS IN: $$TXN_ACTIVE, $$TXN_PARENT_TABLE, $$TXN_PARENT_ID, $$TXN_LABEL
# RETURNS: { ok, reverted, reason }
# GLOBALS OUT: clears $$TXN_*
# SIDE EFFECTS: reverts the parent record and every related record written since txn_Begin
#
# HOW ONE REVERT UNDOES MANY RECORDS:
#   FileMaker holds the open parent AND its related uncommitted records as one editable
#   state. `Revert Record` on the parent throws all of it away. That is the entire
#   pre-2023 transaction pattern — no engine transaction, no rollback log, just the
#   record-locking model used deliberately.
#
#   ⚠️ IT ONLY WORKS IF NOTHING COMMITTED IN BETWEEN. If a routine slipped a
#   `Commit Records` into its loop, those rows are already permanent and this script
#   CANNOT reach them. It will still report ok:true, because from its position nothing is
#   pending. **That is why the no-stray-commit rule is the load-bearing part and this
#   script is only the visible part.**
#
# WHY IT IS DELIBERATELY NOT CLEVER:
#   No compensating deletes, no "find what leaked and clean it up." A repair routine that
#   hunts down half-written rows is a tool for discovering you already sent a wrong payoff
#   quote. Prevention only. If the pattern was violated, the correct outcome is a LOUD
#   failure and a human looking at it — not an automated tidy-up that hides the breach.
#
# 🥇 STATE: GOLDEN — designed, not yet built.
#
# CHANGELOG:
#   - 2026-07-29 designed. Option 3 from DL Q3 (accept non-atomic writes + an after-the-fact
#     repair script) was rejected outright by both Fiona and Dexter, which is why no repair
#     path exists here by design rather than by omission.

# --- SETUP ---
Set Error Capture [ On ]
Set Variable [ $p ; Value: Get ( ScriptParameter ) ]
Set Variable [ $reason ; Value: Case ( IsEmpty ( JSONGetElement ( $p ; "reason" ) ) ; "unspecified" ; JSONGetElement ( $p ; "reason" ) ) ]

# --- A rollback with no open transaction is a CALLER BUG, and saying so is the point.
#     Silently returning ok would hide a routine that thinks it is protected. ---
If [ $$TXN_ACTIVE <> 1 ]
	Exit Script [ Result: JSONSetElement ( "{}" ; [ "ok" ; False ; JSONBoolean ] ; [ "error" ; "txn_Rollback called with no open transaction — caller is not using the wrapper correctly" ; JSONString ] ) ]
End If

# --- REVERT ON THE PARENT ---
Go to Layout [ $$TXN_PARENT_TABLE ]
Enter Find Mode [ Pause: Off ]
Set Field By Name [ $$TXN_PARENT_TABLE & "::PrimaryKey" ; $$TXN_PARENT_ID ]
Perform Find [ ]
Revert Record/Request [ With dialog: Off ]

Set Variable [ $err ; Value: Get ( LastError ) ]

# --- CLEAR STATE regardless: the transaction is over either way, and leaving $$TXN_ACTIVE
#     armed would block the next txn_Begin with a phantom "already open". ---
Set Variable [ $$TXN_ACTIVE ; Value: "" ]
Set Variable [ $$TXN_ID ; Value: "" ]
Set Variable [ $$TXN_PARENT_TABLE ; Value: "" ]
Set Variable [ $$TXN_PARENT_ID ; Value: "" ]
Set Variable [ $$TXN_LABEL ; Value: "" ]

If [ $err ]
	Exit Script [ Result: JSONSetElement ( "{}" ; [ "ok" ; False ; JSONBoolean ] ; [ "error" ; "revert reported error " & $err & " — INSPECT THE DATA MANUALLY" ; JSONString ] ; [ "reason" ; $reason ; JSONString ] ) ]
End If

Exit Script [ Result: JSONSetElement ( "{}" ; [ "ok" ; True ; JSONBoolean ] ; [ "reverted" ; True ; JSONBoolean ] ; [ "reason" ; $reason ; JSONString ] ) ]

# --- ACCEPTANCE TEST (run this before trusting the wrapper anywhere) ---
# Post RCPT-001 (the two-loan check) with a deliberate failure injected after the FIRST
# PaymentApplications row is written. Correct result: ZERO PaymentApplications rows exist
# and ZERO AccountTransactions rows exist afterward.
# If ONE row survives, a stray commit is still in the routine and the wrapper is decorative.
